cuems-common (1.3.0-10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * cuems-nodeconf.service: drop RemainAfterExit=yes. cuems-nodeconf is now a
    long-running daemon (resident avahi discovery loop + interface-scoped alias
    supervision), not a one-shot. With Type=notify, RemainAfterExit=yes would
    leave the unit 'active (exited)' when the daemon dies, masking the failure
    from dependents and from Restart= monitoring. Pairs with the cuems-nodeconf
    Phase-1 re-enable.

 -- Ion Reguera <ion@stagelab.coop>  Fri, 12 Jun 2026 13:10:00 +0200

cuems-common (1.3.0-9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * rtpmidid.service.d/cuems-node-group.conf: order rtpmidid after avahi-daemon
    (Wants=+After=avahi-daemon.service) so its libavahi-client connects to a
    ready daemon and publishes its _apple-midi services. On cold boot rtpmidid
    was starting ~170ms before avahi-daemon, deferring its registration and
    (pre rtpmidid 26.06~1stagelab1) never committing it -> controller invisible
    over mDNS, nodes never discover its MTC export, nodes get no MTC. Pairs with
    the rtpmidid announce_all()-on-S_RUNNING fix. Deliberately avoids
    network-online.target (documented ordering-cycle trap above the new lines).

 -- Ion Reguera <ion@stagelab.coop>  Thu, 04 Jun 2026 18:40:00 +0200

cuems-common (1.3.0-8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * sudoers/99-cuems-poweroff: fix the systemctl path /sbin/systemctl ->
    /usr/bin/systemctl. /sbin/systemctl does not exist on bookworm (usr-merge),
    so the two `cuems ... /sbin/systemctl poweroff[ --no-block]` entries never
    matched and were dead. The working /sbin/poweroff entry (what the bridge
    actually uses) was unaffected; this only matters if controller_poweroff_cmd
    is set to a systemctl invocation.

 -- Ion Reguera <ion@stagelab.coop>  Thu, 04 Jun 2026 13:41:10 +0200

cuems-common (1.3.0-7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * sudoers: add 99-cuems-admin-poweroff granting cuems-admin passwordless
    /sbin/poweroff. cuems-power-bridge now SSHes nodes as cuems-admin (which
    has a login shell) instead of the nologin cuems service user; this lets
    the unattended, no-TTY poweroff complete. Scoped to poweroff only — full
    cuems-admin sudo still requires a password. New file (not an edit of
    99-cuems-poweroff) to avoid a conffile prompt on upgrade. The bridge key
    is additionally locked to this one command via a forced-command entry in
    cuems-admin's authorized_keys (shipped by cuems-power-bridge deploy-keys).

